<%# One reading measure for the long legal texts (owner's repair request of
    2026-10-21, item 4). A judgment's and a parliamentary document's full text
    ran the whole card, 1,200 px at a 1,280 px window, and stayed justified on
    a 390 px phone, where a line holds too few words to spread and the gaps
    stretch. They now keep 80 characters a line (WCAG 1.4.8), justify only from
    40rem, and hyphenate in the language of the text (the container carries its
    lang). A law's articles keep their width (their column also holds the open
    contents and the tables) and only stop justifying on a phone. No
    text-align-last: it is inherited, and set on the container it pulled every
    centred one-line child (the judgment's masthead) to the start; a justified
    paragraph's last line starts naturally without it. These rules are
    unlayered, so they win over the stylesheet's @layer base without an asset
    build.
    Wide (owner's request of 2026-11-06, via Sol): at 1,550 px the 80 characters
    used 690 of the card's 1,456 px. From 80rem the text is 64rem wide unless
    the reader chose Comfortable (reading_width_controller.js), and the column
    is centred at both widths. The choice shows only from 80rem and only with
    JavaScript; below 80rem both widths are the same. %>
<% content_for :head do %>
<style>
  .reading-body {
    max-width: 80ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: start;
    hyphens: auto;
    -webkit-hyphens: auto;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .article-content { text-align: start; }
  .reading-width-control { display: none; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .reading-body,
    .article-content {
      text-align: justify;
      text-justify: inter-word;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 80rem) {
    .reading-body:not([data-reading-width="comfortable"]) { max-width: min(100%, 64rem); }
    .reading-width-control:not([hidden]) { display: inline-flex; }
  }
</style>
<% end %>
